    "title": "Kohl’s Shares Store Closures Update After Shutting 27 Locations",
    "text": "Key PointsKohl’s will not close additional stores in 2026 despite recent sales declines.CEO Michael J. Bender emphasizes optimizing and improving existing stores, not expanding or downsizing.The company aims for stability and operational strength after a year of restructuring. After announcing plans to shut down dozens of locations last year, Kohl’s is now offering a clearer picture of what’s ahead. Despite ongoing sales declines, the retailer says it does not expect to close additional stores in 2026. The update comes after Kohl’s revealed in January 2025 that it would close 27 stores across 15 states, a move that raised concerns about the brand’s long-term footprint. Now, company leadership is seemingly shifting their focus. “I would not anticipate any sort of grand plan of saying we’re taking stores out or adding stores at this point,” Kohl’s Chief Executive Officer Michael J. Bender said per the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel on Tuesday, March 17. “The focus for us is actually on optimizing what we already have and we’ll be focused on making sure that we continue to push the store’s productivity going forward.” Instead of downsizing further, Kohl’s appears to be doubling down on improving its existing locations. According to Bender, the retailer still operates around 1,150 stores, with more than 90 percent currently profitable. YesYes75%NoNo25% But Kohl’s latest earnings report, released March 10, showed a dip in performance. Fourth-quarter net sales fell 3.9 percent, while comparable sales dropped 2.8 percent. For the full fiscal year, net sales decreased 4.0 percent, with comparable sales down 3.1 percent. Even so, executives say the company is in a better place than it was a year ago. “We are ending 2025 in a stronger position than we started, with important work still ahead of us,” Bender said, noting that recent efforts have focused on “resetting” the company’s foundation. Looking ahead, Kohl’s says its priority is stability, not expansion or more closures. The company’s broader plan is “intended to stabilize the business and strengthen our operational ability to build for a stronger future,” Bender added. About the authorJane LaCroix is a news writer specializing in celebrity news, entertainment, and travel/culture. With over a decade of experience, she focuses on reporting trending news that resonates with readers with accuracy and clarity.",
